<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/snow-house.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/snow-house-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/snow-house.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="900" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><a href="/appraisal-work/">Appraisal work</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><span aria-current="page">Homeowner Claim Appraisal</span></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal work</p><h1>Homeowner Claim Appraisal for Maine Homes</h1><p class="lede">A homeowner claim appraisal in Maine starts with one question: will the insurer’s check actually put your house back the way it was? For capes, connected farmhouses, lake camps and Victorians, the answer is often no, and appraisal is how you fix the number.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-coral" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg><span>Call (844) 537-1427</span></a><a class="btn btn-line light" href="/reach-us/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="spec"><div><dt>Common homes</dt><dd>Capes, colonials, camps</dd></div><div><dt>Typical issue</dt><dd>Matching and access costs</dd></div><div><dt>Our role</dt><dd>Your named appraiser</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><h2>Why Maine houses are hard to price from a desk</h2>
<p>Carrier estimating software is built on regional averages. Maine houses are rarely average. A connected farmhouse in Kennebec County may run big house, little house, back house and barn under one continuous roofline, so a leak in the ell touches framing that dates from three different centuries. A West End Portland Victorian has plaster, crown and window trim that cannot be swapped for stock lumber. A lake camp in Piscataquis County may have no road access after the first snow. A homeowner claim appraisal in Maine works because it replaces averages with the actual building.</p>

<h2>What carrier estimates commonly miss on homes</h2>
<p>When we compare a homeowner’s estimate against the carrier’s, the gaps fall into predictable groups:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Matching.</strong> Discontinued vinyl colors, cedar shingles that have weathered gray, and painted clapboard that cannot be blended at a single wall.</li>
<li><strong>Hidden damage.</strong> Wet insulation, swollen subfloor and mold behind finished walls that was not opened during a short inspection.</li>
<li><strong>Code and permit items.</strong> Ice and water shield, electrical updates or egress requirements triggered by the repair, where your policy includes ordinance or law coverage.</li>
<li><strong>Access and season.</strong> Ferry fares and barge time for island homes, winter protection, and the premium contractors charge after a big storm.</li>
<li><strong>Overhead and profit.</strong> Left off even when the job plainly needs a general contractor to coordinate several trades.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How homeowner appraisal works from start to finish</h2>
<p>Once the dispute is about amount, either side sends a written demand under the policy. You name us as your appraiser; the carrier names its own. We inspect, write a complete estimate and meet the other appraiser to work through every disputed line. Most homeowner files settle at that stage. If the two appraisers cannot agree, the umpire reviews the differences and an award signed by two of the three sets the amount. Our <a href="/the-appraisal-path/">appraisal path guide</a> lays out each step and the paperwork involved.</p>

<h3>Illustrative example</h3>
<p>Illustrative example: a carrier pays $18,400 for interior water damage to a 1920s cape in Brunswick, pricing drywall and builder-grade trim. The owner’s scope adds plaster repair, matching fir flooring across two connected rooms and a winter heat-and-dry allowance, totaling $31,900. The difference of $13,500 is a pricing and scope disagreement, the kind appraisal is meant to resolve. These figures are invented to show the arithmetic, not a result.</p>

<div class="note"><b class="h">Depreciation and holdback</b><p>Many Maine policies pay actual cash value first and release the depreciation holdback once repairs are done. Make sure any award separates replacement cost from actual cash value so you know what you will receive and when. The <a href="/claim-math/rcv-acv-holdback-calculator/">RCV and ACV holdback calculator</a> shows how the pieces fit.</p></div>

<h2>When a homeowner should call a lawyer instead</h2>
<p>If the insurer says the loss is excluded, accuses you of misrepresentation, or says a vacant or unoccupied house is not covered, you have a coverage problem, not an amount problem. Appraisal does not decide coverage. A Maine attorney can advise you on that and on any policy deadline. Seasonal owners should also check the <a href="/claim-math/seasonal-camp-vacancy-check/">seasonal camp vacancy check</a>, since vacancy and freeze-protection conditions often drive coverage questions on camps and second homes.</p>

<h2>Getting started</h2>
<p>Send us the policy, the carrier’s estimate and your photos. We will tell you honestly whether the gap is worth pursuing, what appraisal will cost, and what we would need to see before naming ourselves as your appraiser.</p>
<h2>Questions about homeowner claim appraisal</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Is homeowner claim appraisal in Maine only for big losses?</summary><p>No, but the gap should be large enough to justify the appraiser’s fee and a share of the umpire. We help you run that math before you commit.</p></details><details><summary>Will my insurer cancel my policy if I demand appraisal?</summary><p>Appraisal is a right written into the policy. If you are worried about nonrenewal, you can ask the Maine Bureau of Insurance about the rules that apply to your situation.</p></details><details><summary>Can appraisal include contents as well as the house?</summary><p>Often yes, if the policy’s appraisal clause covers the whole amount of loss. Contents files need good inventories, receipts or photos to support value.</p></details><details><summary>My camp is on an island. Will the appraisal account for that?</summary><p>Yes. Ferry or barge costs, crew travel time and limited working windows are real costs on island and remote properties, and we price them into the estimate.</p></details></div>
